Step 7 Socratic Question: What should I check to avoid the mistake of circular logic, which is drawing conclusions in advance into assumptions? Pause and observe the step carefully: pages 15 to 17. Since the two sides AB and AC are the same (assumed), the green line segment AD in the middle is the common side, and half of the vertex angle, which is the included angle, is equal to each other, the two triangles ABD and ACD are SAS congruent, and the principle that the two base angles, which are corresponding angles, are the same in size is completed. What you must remember at this time is that in order to avoid using the conclusion to be proved (the two base angles are equal) in advance during the proof process, you must draw an auxiliary line bisector of the vertex angles and derive the SAS congruence to avoid falling into circular logic.

